mc(3, 'hypothalamus', 'Which of these is a hypothalamic hormone that inhibits a pituitary hormone?', 'somatostatin', ['GnRH', 'oxytocin', 'TSH'], 'Somatostatin inhibits the release of growth hormone. GnRH is a releasing hormone, and oxytocin is made in the hypothalamus but acts on the uterus.');
mc(4, 'adrenal', 'A tumour of the adrenal medulla could cause', 'attacks of palpitations, sweating and raised blood pressure', ['low blood sodium and weakness', 'tetany', 'goitre'], 'It would release too much adrenaline and noradrenaline. Low Na<sup>+</sup> is a feature of low aldosterone.');
mc(4, 'adrenal', 'The same molecule can act as a hormone and as a neurotransmitter. This is true for', 'noradrenaline', ['insulin', 'cortisol', 'thyroxine'], 'Noradrenaline is secreted by the adrenal medulla as a hormone and by sympathetic nerve endings as a neurotransmitter.');
mc(4, 'pituitary', 'A cut across the pituitary stalk will most strongly affect the secretion of', 'ADH (vasopressin) and oxytocin from the posterior pituitary', ['insulin', 'thyroxine only', 'cortisol only'], 'The neurosecretory axons run in the stalk. Without them, ADH and oxytocin cannot reach the posterior pituitary, so the patient develops diabetes insipidus.', 'The anterior pituitary is also affected as the portal vessels are cut.');
mc(4, 'parathyroid', 'Low blood calcium raises PTH. Which set of events follows?', 'bone resorption, renal reabsorption of Ca<sup>2+</sup> and greater gut absorption', ['bone deposition, renal excretion and lower gut absorption', 'bone resorption and renal excretion', 'only greater gut absorption'], 'PTH raises Ca<sup>2+</sup> by three routes, all of which raise blood calcium.');
mc(4, 'feedback', 'A person has high blood Ca<sup>2+</sup>. Which hormone change helps to correct it?', 'More TCT and less PTH', ['More PTH and less TCT', 'More insulin', 'More aldosterone'], 'TCT lowers calcium by suppressing bone resorption. PTH would raise it.');
mc(4, 'mechanism', 'A hormone H binds a membrane receptor and increases cyclic AMP in the cell. H could be', 'glucagon', ['cortisol', 'testosterone', 'thyroxine'], 'Glucagon is a peptide hormone that uses a second messenger. The others bind intracellular receptors.');
mc(4, 'pancreas', 'A person has too little insulin. The best description of the blood and urine is', 'high blood glucose, with glucose and ketone bodies in the urine', ['low blood glucose, with no glucose in the urine', 'high blood calcium and ketones absent', 'low blood glucose, with ketone bodies in the urine'], 'Without insulin, cells cannot take up glucose. The fats are used and ketone bodies are made, and glucose appears in the urine.');
mc(5, 'adrenal', 'Which of the following is NOT an action of glucocorticoids?', 'stimulating the uptake of glucose by muscle and fat cells', ['stimulating gluconeogenesis', 'suppressing the immune response', 'stimulating RBC production'], 'Glucose uptake into cells is the job of insulin. Glucocorticoids raise blood glucose.');
mc(5, 'hypothalamus', 'The hypothalamus controls the pituitary. Which statement is correct?', 'It controls the anterior pituitary with releasing and inhibiting hormones, and the posterior pituitary through nerve fibres', ['It controls both lobes by portal vessels only', 'It controls both lobes through nerve fibres only', 'It does not control the posterior lobe'], 'The anterior lobe is linked by a portal circulation. The posterior lobe is the end of hypothalamic axons.');
mc(5, 'feedback', 'A person has high TSH, low T<sub>4</sub> and a swollen neck. A reasonable cause is', 'iodine deficiency', ['a tumour of the thyroid making hormone', 'a damaged hypothalamus', 'excess GH'], 'Low T<sub>4</sub> removes negative feedback, so TSH rises and the gland grows (goitre). In a thyroid tumour, T<sub>4</sub> would be high and TSH low.');
mc(5, 'hormones', 'Which of the following hormones have the same type of chemical nature?', 'Oestradiol, progesterone and cortisol', ['Insulin, cortisol and adrenaline', 'Thyroxine, glucagon and aldosterone', 'Adrenaline, oestradiol and TSH'], 'All three are steroids. The other sets mix peptides, steroids, amino acid derivatives or iodothyronines.');
mc(5, 'gonads', 'Which pair of a cell and its hormone is correct?', 'Leydig cell – androgens; corpus luteum – progesterone', ['Leydig cell – oestrogen; corpus luteum – androgen', 'Sertoli cell – progesterone; follicle – testosterone', 'Leydig cell – progesterone; follicle – androgen'], 'Leydig cells of the testes make androgens. The corpus luteum, formed after ovulation, makes progesterone.');

